(TIM-15 - Wait and Sleep really do pause: a timer wrapped round them
 reports at least the requested duration. One second requested, so at
 least 1000 milliseconds measured, and the 250-millisecond sleep on top
 of it takes the total past 1250.
 Also covers: TIM-21 in part (a timer measures a real interval).
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox TIM-15.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   waiting one second took at least a second
   the extra 250 millisecond sleep was taken too
)
a timer called clock.
Start the clock.
Wait 1 second.
a number called 'after the wait' is the clock's elapsed in milliseconds.
Sleep for 250 milliseconds.
Stop the clock.
a number called 'after the sleep' is the clock's duration in milliseconds.
If 'after the wait' is greater than 999 then, print "waiting one second took at least a second".
Otherwise, print "waiting one second took only {'after the wait'} milliseconds".
If 'after the sleep' is greater than 1249 then, print "the extra 250 millisecond sleep was taken too".
Otherwise, print "the whole job took only {'after the sleep'} milliseconds".
